a conservative judge who is sanctioned  by the u.s for alleged human rights  abuses  won iran's presidential election today  he would replace a moderate the change  comes as iran and the u.s are  negotiating the terms of restoring a  2015 agreement  to limit iran's nuclear and ballistic  missile programs in exchange for the  lifting of u.s economic sanctions  elizabeth palmer reports from tehran  ibrahim raisi a cleric and hard-line  judge will be the new president of iran  on the surface the election looked like  any well-organized democratic contest  some voters were happy to discuss their  choice is everybody in your family going  to  vote for mr ricey yes did you discuss it  before  yes we talked about that  [Laughter]  others prefer to keep it a secret you  won't tell me  no you voted for  the candidate who will give you a better  life  government ads has stressed polling  stations would be coveted safe  and urged people to come to the polls  but everybody knows this was no open  contest 600 candidates applied to run  for president  iran's religious authorities picked  seven  not one of them offering true reform  millions of would-be voters decided  there was just no point  what are you going to do on election day  i sleep in a home  there is huge disillusionment with  iran's islamic regime  i did for 42 years  i didn't see anything to head off  an embarrassingly low turnout election  officials kept polling stations open for  an  extra six hours they finally closed  at 2 a.m  in spite of all that turnout was the  lowest it's been  since the revolution rye ec won by a  landslide  but in fact only roughly half of  eligible voters  bothered to cast a ballot for cbs this  morning saturday  i'm elizabeth palmer in tehran
 

